.TH EUCLID-CLI-EMM-IMPORT 1 "August 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-emm-import \- import a JSON file written by "emm export" back into MongoDB
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli
[\fB\-\-endpoint\fR \fIURL\fR]
.B emm import
.BR \-\-file | \-f
.I PATH
[\fB\-\-module\fR|\fB\-m\fR \fIMODULE\fR[,\fIMODULE\fR...]]
[\fB\-\-passphrase\fR [\fIPHRASE\fR]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Imports a JSON file previously written by
.BR euclid-cli-emm-export (1)
back into MongoDB - the counterpart to
.BR export ,
and, like it, a lightweight tool rather than a full
.BR mongorestore (1)
replacement.
.PP
Every document in the file is upserted into its collection, matched by
its
.I _id
- if a document with that
.I _id
already exists it is fully
.B replaced
(not merged: fields present in the live document but absent from the
imported one are removed, so the result matches the export exactly),
otherwise it is inserted. The caller must be authenticated as an
administrator; see
.BR euclid-cli-emm (1)
for how that's checked.
.PP
Only collection names the server recognizes as belonging to a module
(the same set
.B export
draws from) are ever written to; anything else found in the file's
.I collections
object is reported back as skipped instead of being written to an
arbitrary collection.
.PP
By default every collection present in the file is imported.
.B \-\-module
restricts this to a comma-separated subset of the file's modules - e.g.
re-importing only
.I esm
out of a file that
.B export \-\-all
produced.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-file " " \fIPATH\fR
JSON file previously written by
.BR euclid-cli-emm-export (1).
Required.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-module " " \fIMODULE\fR[,\fIMODULE\fR...]
Comma-separated subset of the file's modules to import, e.g.
.IR esm,eqs .
Optional; when omitted, every collection in the file is imported. Applies
to a sealed archive as well, which is filtered after it is opened.
.TP
.B \-\-passphrase " " [\fIPHRASE\fR]
Passphrase the archive was sealed with, for a file written by
.B "emm export \-\-passphrase"
- required for such a file and refused for any other, since a passphrase
means nothing to an archive that has none.
.IP
Given without a value, it is asked for on the terminal without echoing,
which keeps it out of the shell's history; reading it from a pipe works
too.
.IP
A wrong passphrase and an altered file fail identically - the archive is
authenticated as it is decrypted, so neither can be told from the other -
and in both cases nothing is written: an archive is opened whole before
any of it reaches the database.
.SH OUTPUT
The JSON response is printed to standard output:
.RS
.TP
.I imported
Object keyed by collection name, each value
.RI { imported: " " count "[, " failed: " " count ] }
- the number of documents upserted, and, if any were skipped because
they couldn't be parsed, how many.
.TP
.I skipped
Array of collections in the file that weren't imported, each with a
.I reason
- an unrecognized collection name, or one excluded by
.BR \-\-module .
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The request was accepted and processed; check the printed
.I imported / skipped
summary for per-collection results (a collection-level parse failure
or an unrecognized/filtered-out collection does not itself fail the
command - see OUTPUT).
.TP
.B 1
Missing/invalid arguments, the file doesn't exist, isn't valid JSON, or
has neither a
.I collections
object nor
.IR frames ,
a sealed archive was given without
.B \-\-passphrase
(or an unsealed one with it), the passphrase does not open the archive,
the stored session is missing or not an administrator's, or the request
could not be sent or was rejected by the server.
.SH EXAMPLES
Round-trip a module through export and import:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 emm export --module eqs --file eqs-backup.json
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 emm import --file eqs-backup.json
.fi
.RE
.PP
Re-import only ENS out of a multi-module export:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 emm import --file all-backup.json --module ens
.fi
.RE
.PP
Open an archive sealed by
.BR euclid-cli-emm-export (1),
being asked for the passphrase:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 emm import \e
    --file euclid-backup.json --passphrase
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-emm (1),
.BR euclid-cli-emm-export (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-login (1)
